WEBVTT

00:00.517 --> 00:05.844
Picture this. Kamla is fifty. She lives in a
village in Haryana, and most mornings

00:05.844 --> 00:08.572
she is out working in her family's fields.

00:08.960 --> 00:13.562
For a few months now, she has been waking in
the night, drenched in sweat.

00:13.562 --> 00:17.790
And lately she keeps thinking of her mother,
who had a bone disease.

00:18.178 --> 00:22.915
She hasn't mentioned it to anyone. It
doesn't feel like something you talk about,

00:22.915 --> 00:25.663
and the nearest doctor is a long bus ride
away.

00:26.050 --> 00:31.685
Kamla is made up. But women like her are
not, and one of them may be closer to you

00:31.685 --> 00:32.716
than you think.

00:34.467 --> 00:40.012
Think of an older woman in your family. Your
mother, an aunt, a grandmother.

00:40.012 --> 00:42.419
At what age did her periods stop?

00:43.884 --> 00:45.104
Most of us don't know.

00:45.491 --> 00:50.101
And here is a harder question. Did anyone
ever ask her?

00:50.101 --> 00:52.783
Her doctor? Her own mother? You?

00:54.248 --> 00:57.961
Around that age, a lot was changing inside
her body.

00:57.961 --> 00:59.746
Most of it made no sound.

01:01.497 --> 01:04.476
Across India, there are millions of women
like her.

01:04.864 --> 01:07.673
Indian women reach menopause at about
forty-six.

01:07.673 --> 01:11.593
That is roughly five years earlier than
women in Western countries.

01:11.981 --> 01:16.767
And many have a hysterectomy, an operation
to remove the womb, in their late

01:16.767 --> 01:17.334
thirties.

01:17.723 --> 01:23.671
Now, the changes that made no sound. As her
hormones fall, LDL, low-density

01:23.671 --> 01:29.063
lipoprotein or the bad cholesterol, rises,
and her arteries stiffen.

01:29.450 --> 01:31.092
Bone loss speeds up.

01:31.480 --> 01:37.181
Genitourinary symptoms begin, such as
vaginal dryness and urinary urgency.

01:37.570 --> 01:42.391
And she faces decisions about hormone
therapy, which replaces the hormones the

01:42.391 --> 01:45.173
ovaries stop making, and about breast
cancer.

01:46.927 --> 01:52.212
How much help does she get? Breast-screening
coverage in India is about two percent:

01:52.212 --> 01:53.407
one woman in fifty.

01:53.795 --> 01:57.716
Most midlife women never discuss these
changes with anyone.

01:58.102 --> 02:03.793
So who is nearby? Often an ASHA, an
Accredited Social Health Activist: a
community

02:03.793 --> 02:07.402
health worker under India's National Health
Mission.

02:07.791 --> 02:12.409
In rural areas, there is roughly one ASHA
for every thousand people.

02:12.796 --> 02:18.214
These are the women in our own families, and
many of these changes can be managed if

02:18.214 --> 02:19.762
someone notices in time.

02:21.527 --> 02:27.006
This idea did not start with menopause. In
Phase One, the team built Palli Sahayak,

02:27.006 --> 02:30.306
an open-source voice helpline for palliative
care.

02:30.694 --> 02:35.011
People phone in and talk in their own
language, in any of the twenty-two languages

02:35.011 --> 02:37.485
of the Eighth Schedule of India's
Constitution.

02:37.873 --> 02:44.142
Voice matters. It needs no reading, no
typing, no smartphone and no internet.

02:44.529 --> 02:49.696
Experts reviewed it one hundred and sixty
times: an average of seven point eight out

02:49.696 --> 02:53.572
of ten, and fifty-nine percent endorsed it
without reservation.

02:53.959 --> 03:00.000
Most failures came in difficult escalation
cases: the moments when a caller needed a

03:00.000 --> 03:00.432
human.

03:00.819 --> 03:04.781
And experts who had not been calibrated
disagreed sharply.

03:04.781 --> 03:08.812
If two experts agreed, their scores would
sit on this line.

03:08.812 --> 03:09.632
They didn't.

03:11.397 --> 03:16.721
That is why we chose this problem. Midlife
women's health is large and neglected,

03:16.721 --> 03:21.980
and the same voice engine could reach women
like Kamla, on the phone she already

03:21.980 --> 03:22.243
has.

03:22.630 --> 03:28.429
So here is the question. Could a voice like
this, one she can simply call, help a

03:28.429 --> 03:30.004
woman through midlife?

03:31.469 --> 03:36.199
And could it do that safely, knowing what it
must never get wrong?

03:37.665 --> 03:43.591
This is Vaani Sakhi. Vaani means voice.
Sakhi is the Hindi word for a woman's

03:43.591 --> 03:44.746
trusted friend.

03:45.133 --> 03:50.351
It is deliberately narrow, and we will be
clear about what it promises and what it

03:50.351 --> 03:51.305
only hopes for.

03:53.067 --> 03:59.201
Why not just ask a chatbot? Chatbots run on
a large language model, or LLM, which

03:59.201 --> 04:00.715
answers from memory.

04:01.103 --> 04:07.086
On menopause questions, off-the-shelf LLMs
answer only thirty to seventy percent

04:07.086 --> 04:08.283
fully correctly.

04:08.672 --> 04:13.913
Fix one: look it up first. In
retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, the
model

04:13.913 --> 04:18.158
fetches passages from trusted documents,
then answers from them.

04:18.545 --> 04:23.825
Hybrid RAG searches two ways at once: by
keyword, and by meaning.

04:24.213 --> 04:29.661
Fix two: use a calculator, don't guess. With
tool calling, the model runs our own

04:29.661 --> 04:35.176
software and uses the result. Tool-calling
agents reach about eighty-eight percent

04:35.176 --> 04:36.857
on clinical calculations.

04:37.244 --> 04:43.052
Fix three: know when to stop. A warning sign
sends the call to a human, exactly

04:43.052 --> 04:44.963
where Phase One struggled.

04:46.717 --> 04:51.342
Let's build Vaani Sakhi piece by piece,
starting with a call.

04:51.342 --> 04:52.555
Say it is Kamla.

04:52.943 --> 04:59.212
[Kamla, in Haryanvi] I sweat a lot at night,
and my mother had a bone disease.

04:59.212 --> 05:00.418
Should I worry?

05:00.935 --> 05:06.589
She calls from a basic feature phone over an
ordinary line, in Hindi or English.

05:06.589 --> 05:07.932
No internet needed.

05:08.320 --> 05:14.018
Her voice reaches the core: Google's Gemini
Live API, or application programming

05:14.018 --> 05:18.790
interface, a real-time speech-to-speech AI
model she can interrupt.

05:19.178 --> 05:25.154
And here is the rule that matters most. It
never answers clinical questions from the

05:25.154 --> 05:26.506
model's own memory.

05:26.894 --> 05:31.353
Clinical statements come only from
guidelines that clinicians have signed off.

05:31.353 --> 05:34.212
Risk numbers come only from validated
calculators.

05:35.967 --> 05:39.465
To keep that rule, the core calls four
tools.

05:39.853 --> 05:45.755
The first, retrieve guideline, searches a
knowledge base of Indian and international

05:45.755 --> 05:46.528
guidelines.

05:46.916 --> 05:52.130
The guidelines become a knowledge graph,
linking each condition, symptom and rule,

05:52.130 --> 05:55.310
so night sweats lead to the rules that cover
them.

05:55.698 --> 06:00.986
Every passage carries its source, date and
evidence grade: how strong the research

06:00.986 --> 06:01.824
behind it is.

06:02.211 --> 06:06.619
The second, compute risk, uses exactly three
validated calculators.

06:06.619 --> 06:11.949
The World Health Organization's South-Asia
chart gives the ten-year risk of heart

06:11.949 --> 06:14.515
attack or stroke, without a blood test.

06:14.903 --> 06:20.084
FRAX-India, the Fracture Risk Assessment
Tool set to Indian fracture rates, gives

06:20.084 --> 06:22.963
the ten-year chance of a major bone
fracture.

06:23.351 --> 06:27.963
And the Menopause Rating Scale scores her
symptoms with eleven questions.

06:28.350 --> 06:31.568
Notice what is missing: a breast-cancer risk
score.

06:31.568 --> 06:36.238
None is validated in Indian women, so a
family-history rule refers her for

06:36.238 --> 06:37.437
assessment instead.

06:37.826 --> 06:43.154
The third, check red flags, looks for
warning signs: bleeding after menopause, a

06:43.154 --> 06:48.283
breast lump, heart-disease warning signs in
women, or a mental-health crisis.

06:48.671 --> 06:52.322
When a red flag appears, the path turns red.

06:52.710 --> 06:58.161
And the fourth, handoff, asks the site
clinician or her ASHA to call her back, and

06:58.161 --> 06:59.490
texts her a summary.

07:01.247 --> 07:06.213
Two modes share one engine. In woman mode,
it helps her with self-care and with

07:06.213 --> 07:08.161
preparing for a doctor's visit.

07:08.549 --> 07:12.643
In ASHA assist mode, it supports an ASHA
during home visits.

07:12.643 --> 07:14.417
The engine stays the same.

07:14.805 --> 07:20.189
Clinicians stay in the loop. At partner
sites, still to be decided, they co-write

07:20.189 --> 07:22.250
and sign off the care pathways.

07:22.637 --> 07:28.436
They do it in Delphi rounds: experts rate
statements anonymously and revise, round

07:28.436 --> 07:30.557
after round, until they agree.

07:30.946 --> 07:36.325
If Gemini Live is down, slow or weak in a
dialect, calls switch to Phase One's

07:36.325 --> 07:41.498
Sarvam AI speech pipeline and a model hosted
in India, with the same tools.

07:41.885 --> 07:46.927
Privacy: personal details stay in India.
Names and phone numbers are removed before

07:46.927 --> 07:48.931
anything reaches the cloud model.

07:49.319 --> 07:54.043
Every answer is logged with the versions of
the model, knowledge base and pathways

07:54.043 --> 07:55.022
that produced it.

07:55.410 --> 08:00.651
The proof of concept uses the Gemini API;
open models hosted in India, such as

08:00.651 --> 08:04.347
MedGemma, will be tested and used only if
they do well.

08:06.117 --> 08:11.491
Now a new rule for the pictures: anything
with a dashed outline is something the

08:11.491 --> 08:14.312
team hopes for, not something it promises.

08:14.700 --> 08:19.839
Menopause shows up differently in every
woman, so generic advice often misses.

08:20.226 --> 08:25.424
The hope is a sakhi that gets to know her:
with her consent, a profile of what she

08:25.424 --> 08:27.769
chooses to share, built across calls.

08:28.158 --> 08:33.838
Her age at menopause, symptom scores over
time, conditions and medicines, and her

08:33.838 --> 08:36.923
context, such as language, work and privacy.

08:37.310 --> 08:42.347
Each call would start from that profile:
fewer repeat questions, and a follow-up on

08:42.347 --> 08:42.954
last time.

08:43.342 --> 08:47.923
The profile would change how the agent
explains, never what the guidelines allow.

08:47.923 --> 08:52.335
It would stay in India, she could delete it
any time, and it would never train

08:52.335 --> 08:52.731
models.

08:53.117 --> 08:58.682
For now, the profile would be designed with
users and tried only on written test

08:58.682 --> 08:59.099
cases.

08:59.487 --> 09:05.241
Underneath sits the hardest problem. Heart,
bone and genitourinary guidelines each

09:05.241 --> 09:08.960
give advice, and it can pull in different
directions.

09:09.348 --> 09:14.964
It has to become one plan she can follow,
fitted to her work, her daily life, and

09:14.964 --> 09:16.905
the conditions she lives in.

09:17.293 --> 09:22.731
How do we combine advice from several organ
systems into one plan that is personal

09:22.731 --> 09:25.119
to her? That question is still open.

09:27.947 --> 09:31.598
So how will the team know it is safe? Three
studies.

09:31.986 --> 09:37.476
Study A checks each calculator against its
official version on at least five hundred

09:37.476 --> 09:42.181
made-up patients, and speech on two hundred
scripted calls per language.

09:42.569 --> 09:48.153
Study B uses one hundred and twenty written
patient cases, called vignettes, in

09:48.153 --> 09:49.425
Hindi and English.

09:49.813 --> 09:55.606
Calibrated clinicians rate the answers, two
ratings for each, and a senior clinician

09:55.606 --> 09:59.882
settles any disagreement: safe, needs a
minor edit, or unsafe.

10:00.269 --> 10:05.361
It is compared with the Phase One engine,
the same Gemini model without tools or

10:05.361 --> 10:08.225
retrieval, and a leading general-purpose
LLM.

10:08.613 --> 10:14.315
Study C is a small supervised pilot with
midlife women and ASHAs, at partner sites

10:14.315 --> 10:19.738
still to be decided. It runs alongside usual
care, never instead of it, with a

10:19.738 --> 10:21.894
clinician reviewing every call.

10:22.283 --> 10:27.644
And the raters are calibrated first: they
score practice cases together until they

10:27.644 --> 10:28.428
score alike.

10:30.177 --> 10:35.430
Two numbers carry much of the safety case.
First, sensitivity: of all real red

10:35.430 --> 10:38.057
flags, what share does the agent catch?

10:38.747 --> 10:43.866
Say there are one hundred real red flags,
and the agent catches ninety-five.

10:43.866 --> 10:46.223
Sensitivity is ninety-five percent.

10:46.912 --> 10:51.522
The target is at least ninety-five percent,
with no emergency left without

10:51.522 --> 10:52.207
escalation.

10:52.595 --> 10:58.866
The second is Cohen's kappa: how much better
than luck the agent and clinicians

10:58.866 --> 11:00.374
agree on risk tier.

11:00.762 --> 11:07.489
Call p o how often they actually agree, and
p e how often they would agree by luck

11:07.489 --> 11:07.981
alone.

11:08.670 --> 11:13.233
Kappa divides the agreement above luck by
the most agreement above luck there could

11:13.233 --> 11:13.398
be.

11:14.087 --> 11:19.626
Say they agree ninety percent of the time,
and luck alone would give fifty.

11:19.626 --> 11:24.721
Then kappa is zero point four over zero
point five: zero point eight.

11:25.410 --> 11:29.379
The target is a kappa of at least zero point
seven.

11:31.147 --> 11:36.518
Other targets: a critical-error rate below
one percent, and at least ninety-five

11:36.518 --> 11:39.808
percent of clinical claims traceable to a
source.

11:40.196 --> 11:44.285
A usability score of at least seventy out of
one hundred.

11:44.285 --> 11:48.733
And speech errors, delay and cost per call,
for each language.

11:49.121 --> 11:54.710
And the headline. In Phase One, fifty-nine
percent of reviews endorsed the answers.

11:54.710 --> 12:00.165
The target now: clinicians judge at least
eighty-five percent of vignette answers

12:00.165 --> 12:00.502
safe.

12:00.891 --> 12:04.940
That eighty-five is a target, not a result.

12:05.328 --> 12:09.989
Health outcomes, such as more women getting
screened, need a larger trial.

12:09.989 --> 12:12.068
This project does not claim them.

12:12.455 --> 12:18.194
A question for you. If you had to pick one
red flag the agent must never miss, which

12:18.194 --> 12:19.629
would it be, and why?

12:22.467 --> 12:27.560
So what does the project promise? On the
left, drawn solid, is what it commits to.

12:27.948 --> 12:31.409
A clinician-signed knowledge base and
pathways.

12:31.409 --> 12:37.080
The voice agent and its four tools.
Failover, and an India-hosted data layer.

12:37.080 --> 12:38.479
Studies A, B and C.

12:38.866 --> 12:42.200
Open-source code, knowledge base and test
sets.

12:42.200 --> 12:47.662
A costed plan for scale. And a regulatory
gap analysis toward approval by the

12:47.662 --> 12:52.627
Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation,
India's device regulator.

12:52.627 --> 12:55.181
Filing for approval is not promised.

12:55.569 --> 13:01.002
On the right, dashed, are stretch goals: a
fully personalised sakhi, a caregiver

13:01.002 --> 13:06.638
mode, another regional language, WhatsApp
voice, links to national health services,

13:06.638 --> 13:07.928
and a larger study.

13:08.316 --> 13:12.180
Any of these is a bonus. Success does not
depend on them.

13:13.937 --> 13:17.684
Back to Kamla, with her night sweats, and
her mother's bone disease.

13:18.072 --> 13:23.006
The agent retrieves the guideline on night
sweats and calls FRAX-India for her

13:23.006 --> 13:28.193
fracture risk. Her family history prompts a
clinician callback, and a text summary

13:28.193 --> 13:28.699
arrives.

13:29.087 --> 13:32.459
At the start, I asked whether anyone had
ever asked her.

13:32.846 --> 13:36.875
For many women like Kamla, that conversation
never happens.

13:36.875 --> 13:41.996
Vaani Sakhi is an attempt to make sure there
is always someone she can ask.

13:42.384 --> 13:45.727
And the first person to ask her could still
be you.
